<?xml version="1.0" ?>
<!DOCTYPE reference
  PUBLIC '-//OASIS//DTD DITA Reference//EN'
  'reference.dtd'>
<!--
	Style Samples.

	One example of every Fi3ldMan-specific class, on one page, so the custom
	formatting can be verified by eye in a single scroll instead of being hunted
	across the publication.

	Every class demonstrated here is defined in template-2026/f13ldman.css and
	nowhere else. Stock Oxygen styling is deliberately absent: this page is for
	checking what *we* contribute, matching the scope of tests/publish/.

	Each sample names its own class, so a reader needs no key. If the label says
	colorRed and the sample beside it is not red, that class is not reaching the
	reader.

	Adding a class here also puts it in front of tests/publish/cascade.spec.js,
	which can only audit declarations that match something in the output. Several
	of these rules matched nothing before this page existed.
-->
<reference id="StyleSamples">
	<title>Style Samples</title>
	<shortdesc>Every Fi3ldMan-specific style, in one place, for visual verification</shortdesc>
	<refbody>
		<section>
			<title/>
			<p>Each row names a class and shows it applied. A sample that looks like
				ordinary body text is a styling failure, not a design choice.</p>

			<p outputclass="h1">Text colours</p>
			<table colsep="1" frame="all" rowsep="1">
				<tgroup cols="2">
					<colspec colname="c1"/>
					<colspec colname="c2"/>
					<tbody>
						<row>
							<entry outputclass="bkDarkGray"><b>Class</b></entry>
							<entry outputclass="bkDarkGray"><b>Sample</b></entry>
						</row>
						<row>
							<entry>colorRed</entry>
							<entry><ph outputclass="colorRed">This text should be red</ph></entry>
						</row>
						<row>
							<entry>colorPink</entry>
							<entry><ph outputclass="colorPink">This text should be pink</ph></entry>
						</row>
						<row>
							<entry>colorBlue</entry>
							<entry><ph outputclass="colorBlue">This text should be blue</ph></entry>
						</row>
						<row>
							<entry>colorGray</entry>
							<entry><ph outputclass="colorGray">This text should be gray</ph></entry>
						</row>
						<row>
							<entry>colorOrange</entry>
							<entry><ph outputclass="colorOrange">This text should be orange</ph></entry>
						</row>
						<row>
							<entry>colorLightBlue</entry>
							<entry><ph outputclass="colorLightBlue">This text should be light blue</ph></entry>
						</row>
						<row>
							<entry>colorDarkBlue</entry>
							<entry><ph outputclass="colorDarkBlue">This text should be dark blue, clearly
									darker than colorBlue above</ph></entry>
						</row>
						<row>
							<entry>colorDarkBrown</entry>
							<entry><ph outputclass="colorDarkBrown">This text should be dark brown</ph></entry>
						</row>
					</tbody>
				</tgroup>
			</table>

			<p outputclass="h1">Cell background colours</p>
			<p>These are the classes that Oxygen 28.1 silently overrode: its main.css
				colours every table cell white at a higher specificity, so each of these
				rules carries !important. If any cell below is white, that fix has been
				lost.</p>
			<table colsep="1" frame="all" rowsep="1">
				<tgroup cols="2">
					<colspec colname="c1"/>
					<colspec colname="c2"/>
					<tbody>
						<row>
							<entry outputclass="bkDarkGray"><b>Class</b></entry>
							<entry outputclass="bkDarkGray"><b>Sample cell</b></entry>
						</row>
						<row>
							<entry>bkLightGray</entry>
							<entry outputclass="bkLightGray">Light gray background</entry>
						</row>
						<row>
							<entry>bkDarkGray</entry>
							<entry outputclass="bkDarkGray">Dark gray background</entry>
						</row>
						<row>
							<entry>bkYellow</entry>
							<entry outputclass="bkYellow">Yellow background</entry>
						</row>
						<row>
							<entry>bkBlue</entry>
							<entry outputclass="bkBlue">Blue background</entry>
						</row>
						<row>
							<entry>bkRed</entry>
							<entry outputclass="bkRed">Red background</entry>
						</row>
						<row>
							<entry>bkGreen</entry>
							<entry outputclass="bkGreen">Green background</entry>
						</row>
					</tbody>
				</tgroup>
			</table>

			<p outputclass="h1">Text emphasis</p>
			<p>bold: <ph outputclass="bold">this text should be heavier than the
					surrounding body text</ph></p>
			<!--
				placeholder is shown in a paragraph, not a table cell, on purpose. Unlike
				the bk* classes above it has no !important, so inside a table it loses to
				Oxygen 28.1's main.css and renders white. Demonstrating it here shows the
				rule working; putting it in a cell would show it failing.
			-->
			<p>placeholder: <ph outputclass="placeholder">this text should sit on a
					yellow highlight</ph></p>

			<p outputclass="h1">Heading classes</p>
			<p>DITA has no inline heading, so headings are paragraphs carrying h1 or h2.
				Both render at 2em; the two classes are interchangeable in appearance and
				differ only in authoring intent.</p>
			<p outputclass="h1">h1 — this line should be large</p>
			<p outputclass="h2">h2 — this line should be the same size as the h1 above</p>

			<p outputclass="h1">Link buttons</p>
			<p>enterBtn turns a link into a full-width grey button in 28px bold Arial.</p>
			<p>
				<xref format="dita" href="./Abbreviations.dita" outputclass="enterBtn">enterBtn — a large button link</xref>
			</p>
			<p>Inside a contents-index table the same class is reduced to 20px, because
				that page carries many of them.</p>
			<table outputclass="contents-index">
				<tgroup cols="3">
					<colspec colname="c1"/>
					<colspec colname="c2"/>
					<colspec colname="c3"/>
					<tbody>
						<row>
							<entry>
								<xref format="dita" href="./Abbreviations.dita" outputclass="enterBtn">Abbreviations</xref>
							</entry>
							<entry>
								<xref format="dita" href="./VanesandCranes.dita" outputclass="enterBtn">Vanes &amp; Cranes</xref>
							</entry>
							<entry>
								<xref format="dita" href="../Welcome.dita" outputclass="enterBtn">Welcome</xref>
							</entry>
						</row>
					</tbody>
				</tgroup>
			</table>

			<!--
				item-list was previously used only in the real publication on the target
				network, so nothing in this repository exercised it and the publish tests
				could not see it. It lays links out as a wrapping row of rounded buttons.
			-->
			<p outputclass="h1">Item list</p>
			<p>item-list wraps its links into a row of rounded buttons rather than
				stacking them.</p>
			<p outputclass="item-list">
				<xref format="dita" href="./Abbreviations.dita">First item</xref>
				<xref format="dita" href="./VanesandCranes.dita">Second item</xref>
				<xref format="dita" href="../Welcome.dita">Third item</xref>
			</p>

			<p outputclass="h1">Sortable table</p>
			<p>Click a header cell to sort. The header row is lighter than a plain
				bkDarkGray cell, and sortable columns show a ▴▾ marker; the column marked
				sorttable_nosort is darker, shows no marker, and does not respond to a
				click.</p>
			<table colsep="1" frame="all" outputclass="sortable" rowsep="1">
				<tgroup cols="3">
					<colspec colname="c1"/>
					<colspec colname="c2"/>
					<colspec colname="c3"/>
					<tbody>
						<row>
							<entry outputclass="bkDarkGray"><b>Sortable</b></entry>
							<entry outputclass="bkDarkGray"><b>Also sortable</b></entry>
							<entry outputclass="bkDarkGray sorttable_nosort"><b>Not sortable</b></entry>
						</row>
						<row>
							<entry>Charlie</entry>
							<entry>30</entry>
							<entry>no sort marker</entry>
						</row>
						<row>
							<entry>Alpha</entry>
							<entry>10</entry>
							<entry>no sort marker</entry>
						</row>
						<row>
							<entry>Bravo</entry>
							<entry>20</entry>
							<entry>no sort marker</entry>
						</row>
					</tbody>
				</tgroup>
			</table>

			<!--
				fullWidthTable and table-separator matched nothing in the published output
				before this page, so tests/publish/cascade.spec.js had no element to audit
				them against. They are exercised here for that reason as much as for the
				visual check.
			-->
			<p outputclass="h1">Full width table and separator rows</p>
			<p>fullWidthTable makes a table span the content area. table-separator
				enlarges a row used to break a table into groups.</p>
			<table colsep="1" frame="all" outputclass="fullWidthTable" rowsep="1">
				<tgroup cols="2">
					<colspec colname="c1"/>
					<colspec colname="c2"/>
					<tbody>
						<row>
							<entry nameend="c2" namest="c1" outputclass="table-separator bkLightGray">
								<b>table-separator — this row should be larger than the rows below</b>
							</entry>
						</row>
						<row>
							<entry>Ordinary row</entry>
							<entry>Ordinary row</entry>
						</row>
						<row>
							<entry>Ordinary row</entry>
							<entry>Ordinary row</entry>
						</row>
					</tbody>
				</tgroup>
			</table>
		</section>
	</refbody>
	<related-links id="related-pages">
		<link href="./Abbreviations.dita">
			<linktext>Abbrevs</linktext>
		</link>
		<link href="../Introduction/ContentsPage.dita">
			<linktext>Contents</linktext>
		</link>
	</related-links>
</reference>
